    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the attacker has a list of common passwords and their hashes (the rainbow table), they can just compare them? so the salt essentially makes the attacker's pre-computed list useless because they don't know what the salt was for each specific user?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly! That is the core reason salts exist: since each user's salt is unique and stored alongside their hash, an attacker cannot reuse a pre-computed rainbow table that was built for a specific salt value. Without knowing the individual salt, the attacker would have to start the slow process of testing every possible password hash from scratch for each user, making large-scale attacks on weak passwords extremely expensive and impractical. It effectively turns a mass-cracking problem into millions of isolated guessing games.",
      "source": "target",
